 its direct sales force. Axcelis Technologies, Inc. was founded in 1978 and is headquartered in Beverly, Massachusetts.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Russell J. Low Ph.D. | CEO, President & Director | Age 54 | Compensation: $1.2M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $573K ranks in the above the sector median among Technology peers, improving at 7%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $73K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $14K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Axcelis Technologies, Inc. is a mid-cap technology business with a $3.1B market cap on $839M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -3% over the past three years and +11% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 46% against a 12% cost of capital \u2014 a 34-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, with gross margins sitting at 41%.","The business throws off $107M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 13 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, with adequate 0.98\u00d7 cash conversion.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 22\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield of 4.0% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 64% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.6406579502319696},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 6.8% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Significant insider selling (net $2.6M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-2578031.32},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Technology peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 7% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cybersecurity liability and talent cost inflation \u2014 a single breach can erase years of goodwill, and the war for engineering talent compresses margins even at scale.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Technology peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Stock Analysts\u2019 Ratings Reiterations for November 6th (ABNB, ACLS, AFRM, ALB, ARLO, ATO, AUTO, AXON, BETA, BTA)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/06/stock-analysts-ratings-reiterations-for-november-6th-abnb-acls-afrm-alb-arlo-ato-auto-axon-beta-bta.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793990688.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Did Earnings Beat Just Shift Axcelis Technologies (ACLS) Investment Narrative?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/semiconductors/n